Cardiac CT Scanning Manual – GE Revolution Apex 256-slice
This manual outlines how to perform, troubleshoot, and evaluate cardiac CT scans on the GE Revolution Apex 256-slice scanner. It is designed for technologists and team members who perform advanced cardiac CT imaging at our facility.
GE Revolution Apex (256-slice, 16 cm detector)
Version: 1.0
Author: Sean Chinery 
Last Updated: 11/10/2025
Core Scanning Workflow
1. Patient Preparation and Setup
•	Instructions: 
•	Generally, the RNs are responsible for setting up the patients to come to the machine for scan. This involves giving beta blockers and nitroglycerin, to lower the heart rate and dilate the vessels respectively. 
•	Tips: 
•	While the patient is on the table if they’re blood pressure can handle it, and the heart rate goes up a bit it would be advantageous to ask an RN to give more metoprolol
•	1 exam can be scanned without RN intervention, commonly called either Left Atrial Appendage, or watchman. 
•	Common Errors and How to Respond: 
•	18g Introcan 3 catheters and or >=20g Nexiva Diffusics IVs are the preferred catheters as the injection rate should be higher than smaller IVs can maintain the pressure. 
2. ECG Lead Placement
•	Instructions: 
•	Appended is a visual for the best location for the leads to be placed on the chest for the steadiest rhythm recording. 
•	Common Errors and How to Respond:
•	When the patient raises their arms while getting on the table sometimes the chest tissue will raise the leads above the collar bone, causing an unusable trace. 
•	The black wires cannot be tangled, or it will cause an unusable trace.
•	Preferably IVY brand lead stickers should be used. 
•	The leads should be placed as close to the ribcage as possible. 
•	Legs and arms uncrossed 
3. How the machine acquires images
In brief a 16cm(256 slice) detector acquires heart CTs in terms of rotations as the exams are performed axial instead of helical. Only a portion of each scan is necessary to reconstruct diagnostic images of structure and coronary arteries. 

If the scan occurs with a heart rate of 60 BPM and a rotation time of 0.234 seconds rotation for a full beat then there will be a total of 4.2 rotations of the tube, we’ll simplify this to 4. Therefore a full heart beat scan will take 1 sec(1000ms) for the total of 4 rotations. The total scan time is then divided by a percentage value for the total scan. Most commonly divided into 5% sections of the heartbeat. The machine requires a half turn of the tube or 180 degrees of travel to reconstruct a single set of 256 images at 0.625mm thickness and spacing. When scanning at a single percentage most often 75% the machine will make a single full rotation in that case because it makes a full rotation it is able to fully reconstruct 3 total scans from the total of 360 degrees. One set will be from 0⁰-180⁰, another 90⁰-240⁰ and a final 180⁰-0⁰, resulting in 3 scans, 70%, 75%, 80%. The full heart beat total of four rotations will be reconstructed in total increments of 5 %, 0%(first R-wave) to 100%(second R-wave), dividing the 4 rotations into 20:180⁰ rotations. Table 1.1. 
