4. Timing of images. 
	The timing for a CT cardiac, for diagnosing coronary arteries and structure of the heart is specific. The range for the least amount of motion in the heart occurs during diastasis, the center of which is around 75%. 
 
Figure 1.3: 60BPM NON-irregular Heartbeat cycle, the first trigger the machine records is on the first R-wave, and the second trigger being the second R-wave, 0-100% respectively. 
Ideally Patients heart rate would be stable in the low 60s or 50s. This gives the longest diastasis without compromising the images. Too fast exhibits varying degrees of difficulty when acquiring images. Too fast and diastasis shrinks relative to the total time between r-waves, high rate variability can increase the radiation or miss the ideal percentages entirely, and irregularities can interrupt the scan before the ideal percentages are imaged. 

