4. Contrast Administration and Timing
•	Instructions: 
•	Patients with a GFR above 30 can be given a routine dose of contrast. 
•	Omnipaque 350mg/dl, the equivalent, or greater concentration of contrast
•	7.0ml/s injection rate for both saline and contrast 
•	Cardiacs are best scanned while the contrast has moved from the right side of the heart to the left side and only the left side. In this ideal timing the right side of the heart does not have a large concentration of contrast causing streak artefact from declining image quality 
•	The correct timing is achieved by using the autotriggering function of the machine scanning. SmartPrep by GE.
•	The threshold trigger is 150HU and there is an 11 sec delay at the nearest heart beat trigger between trigger and scan.
•	150HU is not an empirical delay it is a differential. This means that the scan will not be triggered until 150HU + the locating ROI HU average. Blood in the body for the majority of people is ~40HU, therefore the SmartPrep will trigger at 190HU and NOT the 150HU number that is part of the protocol
•	For the best chances of a well-timed CT, it is best to use an ROI that is approx. 50% of the full width of the aorta. A larger area ROI is more accurate to the reality. In addition, a larger ROI will tend to average out any streak artifacts accidentally beginning the scan earlier. 



•	Tips: 
•	If the test injection is around ~60 the 350mg/dl concentration contrast will unlikely reach or trigger the pressure limiting protocol in the power injector
•	A hand flush should be done initially in the position the injection site will be during the CT exam. This is to eval if the LDA can accept the contrast at the pressures we inject. 
